  "notes": "Composed of {征|せい} (conquer, subjugate) and {伐|ばつ} (attack, punish). Implies a righteous or authoritative campaign against an enemy or rebel. Common in historical texts and folk tales, especially the story of {桃太郎|ももたろう}.\n\nCOMMON COLLOCATIONS:\n- {鬼|おに}{征伐|せいばつ}: demon slaying (as in Momotaro)\n- {蝦夷|えぞ}{征伐|せいばつ}: subjugation of the Ezo people\n- {征伐|せいばつ}に{向|む}かう: to set out on a punitive expedition\n- {征伐|せいばつ}{軍|ぐん}: subjugation army\n\nSIMILAR WORDS:\n- {討伐|とうばつ}: subjugation, suppression — very similar, often interchangeable in historical contexts\n- {征服|せいふく}: conquest — emphasizes bringing under one's rule\n- {平定|へいてい}: pacification — focuses on restoring peace after conquest",
